      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. In that case, let's focus on the technical reasons for the knocking noise.\n\nThe knocking sound in your electric water heater can be caused by a few possible issues:\n\n1. Loose or corroded heating elements: Over time, the heating elements can become loose or corroded, causing a rattling or knocking noise.\n2. Misaligned or loose connections: If the connections between the heating elements and the thermostat are loose or misaligned, it can cause a knocking or rattling noise.\n3. Faulty thermostat: A malfunctioning thermostat can cause the heating elements to vibrate or rattle, resulting in a knocking noise.\n4. Mineral buildup: Mineral deposits can accumulate inside the water heater, causing a knocking or rattling noise as the water flows through the system.\n5. Worn-out or damaged components: Over time, the components inside the water heater can wear out or become damaged, causing a knocking or rattling noise.\n\nTo diagnose the issue, it's best to have a professional plumber inspect the water heater and perform any necessary repairs. They can check the connections, thermostat, and other components to determine the cause of the knocking noise and recommend the necessary repairs.\n\nAnd, as",
